WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.127
- For me, I want, you know, younger kids

00:00:02.127 --> 00:00:05.964
to, you know, when they're growing up
and be able to look at Bones Hyland and his resume,

00:00:06.131 --> 00:00:07.549
I want to be like Bones and
even better than Bones.

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:14.556
- [Announcer:] Bones. Bones!
Backcut for Bones. Oh, spinning around everyone. And he’s

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:17.434
shakin’ folks. Bones are shakin’ and rattlin’.

00:00:18.893 --> 00:00:21.187
Bones. Straight away. Got it.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:25.650
- First thing obviously is the name.
It just kind of sticks out to you.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.570
So give us a little origin story
of how that name all came about.

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:30.739
- When I first came to Delaware,

00:00:31.573 --> 00:00:35.243
they only knew me as the name Carolina
because I was originally

00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:37.328
from North Carolina.
That's where I was born at.

00:00:37.328 --> 00:00:38.496
But I was raised in Delaware.

00:00:38.496 --> 00:00:41.708
But the coach who had discovered
me and picked me,

00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:44.878
like, he picked me up and found me,
you know, brought me to the gym.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:46.504
He started calling me Carolina.

00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:50.008
But, you know, the childhood friends
wanted their own

00:00:50.008 --> 00:00:53.053
name for me, and I always
had a slim frame.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:54.220
I was kind of skinny.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.930
And one is a boy.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.350
He came up with the name Bizzy.
And I had another childhood friend.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.603
She's a girl. And she came up
with the name Bones.

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:03.396
And then I just put two and two together

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:06.524
and just came up with Bizzy Bones,
and ever since then, it just stuck to me.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.277
It fits me so well,
and that's always going to be my name.

00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:11.863
You mentioned the coach,

00:01:12.197 --> 00:01:14.824
when the coach just
kind of found you or whatnot.

00:01:15.116 --> 00:01:18.828
Can you kind of speak to that as well as like opportunities that come, you know?

00:01:18.828 --> 00:01:20.622
Like, when you don't
have certain opportunities,

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:23.249
but then you get a chance like
the coach that came and helped you out.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.584
Talk a little bit about that.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:28.171
- So when I first came from North Carolina, you know, I didn't know

00:01:28.171 --> 00:01:31.508
anybody. I didn’t have no friends. Et
cetera. Et cetera.

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:33.676
But I will go down to the,
you know, this gym—it’s called

00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:35.970
West Inner City Community Center—

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.058
and it’s where everybody came to play
basketball, you know, just have fun there.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.394
And, you know, he saw something in me
as a little kid.

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:45.480
And when he first discovered me, he instantly

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:48.691
started calling me, you know,
Carolina. I introduced him to myself.

00:01:48.691 --> 00:01:51.194
I told him where I came from
and stuff like that.

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:52.445
He go by the name Spoons.

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:55.115
And I'm just like, you know,
he introduced himself to me, too.

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:56.616
He’s like, you know, he's a coach
and stuff like that.

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.326
So I'm like, you know,
we’re gonna have a bond for sure

00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:00.411
because, you know,
he was one of the first person I ever spoke to,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:01.663
you know, coming into the city.

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:06.793
So ever since then, he just been like
a father figure to me, a role model to me.

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:10.588
You know, he taught me so many life
lessons and stuff like that, and

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:13.633
that'll always stick with me to this day.
And he always believed in me.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.177
So it meant a lot to me because
like I said, I didn't know anybody.

00:02:16.177 --> 00:02:19.389
So him taking me under his wing
and showing me the ropes

00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:23.143
as a young teenager and just believing in
me, that meant a lot to me.

00:02:23.393 --> 00:02:25.895
- What is the game of basketball
taught you as well?

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:30.191
- It taught me so many different things
that still to this day,

00:02:30.191 --> 00:02:33.987
I still like, you know, thank the game so
much and I never take for granted because,

00:02:34.445 --> 00:02:37.198
you know, I was in numerous life
situations where basketball

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:38.616
could have been tooken away from me.

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:42.453
And I never want to take basketball
for granted because the moment, you know,

00:02:42.453 --> 00:02:45.123
it was almost tooken away from me,
you know, I cried my eyes out.

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:49.669
And, you know, it made me appreciate
the game even more and thank the lessons

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.589
and adversity and stuff
you go through dealing with the game.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:57.635
It makes you appreciate it even more.
- How much of it wasn't an escape for you?

00:02:57.635 --> 00:03:00.889
You know, how much of it was
that you felt like this is my avenue

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:03.892
that I can completely
pour myself into this

00:03:04.225 --> 00:03:07.604
and give me a whole new life that I could
end up, you know, enjoying and living?

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:11.482
- Man, a lot. Honestly,

00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:16.571
it's like basketball, it’s
that space where, you know,

00:03:16.571 --> 00:03:19.616
you just want to just go into the gym
and lock in, you know, with yourself.

00:03:19.782 --> 00:03:20.700
It helped me, you know,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.287
figuring my own stuff out, you know?
Sometimes you need to be alone by yourself

00:03:24.287 --> 00:03:27.290
and do the thing that you love
and you get, you know, tap into different,

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.251
you know, attributes of yourself
and learning yourself even more.

00:03:30.251 --> 00:03:33.963
And that's what, you know, growing up what I did with myself going into the gym.

00:03:33.963 --> 00:03:37.592
It just meant a lot to me,
just being a gym with friends

00:03:37.592 --> 00:03:40.803
and also be in the gym by myself
because I learn so much about myself.

00:03:41.137 --> 00:03:43.765
- I know that you personally when growing up

00:03:43.973 --> 00:03:48.144
didn’t always have transportation,
didn’t always have the money to get these—

00:03:48.186 --> 00:03:51.606
So, like, even when you think about that
and the conversation around just access

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:55.026
for a lot of young athletes and people
who want to get into sports,

00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:58.863
what is it that you're thinking about when these topics come up?

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:03.368
- Me growing up, I had to, you know,
walk to numerous gyms, 30-, 40-minute

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:06.329
walks just to get to the gym
because of no transportation.

00:04:06.371 --> 00:04:10.333
Sometimes, you know,
it would be a van that come to the school

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.210
after school, you had to sit outside,

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:15.004
and they come pick you up
and take you to the gym because, you know,

00:04:15.338 --> 00:04:18.549
sometimes it was so far.
And this was community service stuff

00:04:18.716 --> 00:04:19.968
that the city was providing.

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:21.094
And I was very thankful for that

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:23.680
because it would be cold outside,
snowing, raining.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.224
You know, you're not going to walk
in the rain like that, but you want to,

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:29.352
you know, you want to get to the gym
and play basketball

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:30.311
and do the thing that you love.

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:34.357
But growing up,
it was some things like Safe Haven.

00:04:34.357 --> 00:04:37.777
That's, like, after hours.
You know, from nine to 12:00 a.m.,

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.070
you know, you've been in a gym.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.113
You know, the

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.824
people that’s working it
telling your parents where you at.

00:04:43.241 --> 00:04:45.493
They feed you. You get to play basketball
and stuff like that.

00:04:45.493 --> 00:04:49.247
But as I got older,
those stuff started to fade away,

00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:51.916
and it was more, like, the violence
and stuff like that.

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:55.753
The murder rate started to go up, and
that's something I want to get back into.

00:04:56.045 --> 00:05:00.049
You know, as I'm in the league now
where I can, you know, I can voice myself

00:05:00.049 --> 00:05:01.175
out to the community

00:05:01.175 --> 00:05:05.263
and try to, you know, voice my opinion and and bring that, you know, that joy back.

00:05:05.263 --> 00:05:07.307
Because when I was growing up,
it was so much joy.

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:09.142
And now you’re seeing kids
fade away in different

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:12.937
areas, going left, going right instead of
just trying to, you know, keep straight.

00:05:12.937 --> 00:05:15.690
And that's something I want to do to help to, you know, give back.

00:05:15.732 --> 00:05:17.775
And just voice my opinion on that.

00:05:18.192 --> 00:05:20.903
- What are some of the things maybe
you're excited about being able to give back

00:05:20.903 --> 00:05:23.906
to your community or just in general,
being able to give it back?

00:05:24.907 --> 00:05:26.284
- Uh, you know, partnerin' with

00:05:26.284 --> 00:05:27.660
Under Armour will be huge for me

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.497
to give back because they give that,
you know, the different level outlets

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.332
where they can, you know,

00:05:32.332 --> 00:05:35.168
help me even get involved
in the community, to even give back.

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:38.504
So, you know, that's another reason
why I'm partnerin' with Under Armour.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:40.256
- We're hooked on this whole rookie class.

00:05:40.256 --> 00:05:41.507
We've loved that we've been able to cover

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.551
for the last couple of years,
so we're really excited.

00:05:43.551 --> 00:05:46.888
Just like your game is so different
than everybody else's.

00:05:46.929 --> 00:05:50.058
I know that there's a lot
that goes into that game,

00:05:50.058 --> 00:05:53.019
and there's a lot of pieces
that contributed to the way you play now.

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:56.689
What really contributed
to, like, making your game what it is today?

00:05:56.898 --> 00:06:00.443
- Growin' up, everybody always asks me,
who I watched and who was, like, a role model

00:06:00.443 --> 00:06:01.652
to me, stuff like that.

00:06:01.652 --> 00:06:02.695
I never really had, like,

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.823
you know, somebody I looked up to,
but I always watched Hot Sauce growin' up.

00:06:06.157 --> 00:06:09.410
And that was somebody,
you know, to this day, you know,

00:06:09.786 --> 00:06:13.873
I just thank him for being even alive.
Just being able to watch him on YouTube,

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:16.584
Like take some his moves, his crossovers
and stuff like that.

00:06:16.584 --> 00:06:18.878
And I always, like,
text him to be like, you know,

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:20.254
'Bro, I used to watch you all the time.'

00:06:20.254 --> 00:06:22.507
He texted me back. That means the most to me.

00:06:22.507 --> 00:06:24.133
I recently got a phone call.

00:06:24.133 --> 00:06:25.343
I was gonna FaceTime with him,

00:06:25.343 --> 00:06:28.888
you know, during the pre-draft process, and I was talkin' to him. I'm like, 'Whoa, that's Hot Sauce?'

00:06:29.138 --> 00:06:31.182
You know, that's like one of
the only person for real I like

00:06:31.182 --> 00:06:34.394
I really get hype over to talk to
because I literally come home

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:37.397
from school, eat a bowl of cereal,
and as I'm eatin' bowl of cereal.

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:39.857
I, like, dance in front in the
mirror with the basketball,

00:06:39.857 --> 00:06:41.359
do the same moves that he doin'.

00:06:41.359 --> 00:06:44.946
And it just gave me so
many different level of like,

00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:47.323
you know, that smoothness,
the skill, that like—

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:50.201
It just made me more comfortable
with myself knowing, like,

00:06:50.326 --> 00:06:52.870
that's the type of game
that I wanna be able to play

00:06:53.037 --> 00:06:56.833
and adapt that into the league and just,
you know, just make that my game

00:06:56.833 --> 00:07:00.294
and add my own flair into it.
So, I definitely—big props to him for sure.

00:07:00.378 --> 00:07:04.924
- You know how hype you got me, man,
cuz I, you know, I'm from Atlanta, Georgia.

00:07:05.174 --> 00:07:07.260
We grew up, Hot Sauce was all we know.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.220
He used to sleep there at Run N' Shoot.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:10.263
We had a Run N' Shoot.

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:12.682
Man, you don't wanna
get me started. That's crazy.

00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:16.144
I was not expacting that answer, though.
I love that shout out to Hot Sauce.

00:07:16.853 --> 00:07:21.190
So I saw you say a couple of weeks ago
that, like, 'I wanna be one of the greats.'

00:07:21.482 --> 00:07:24.444
You know, like, what is it—
what fuels you to be

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:27.780
that great and wanna be a legend?

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.199
Man, you know, just to want
my name to be remembered forever.

00:07:30.241 --> 00:07:32.243
I don't wanna just be in this league
and just be somebody,

00:07:32.243 --> 00:07:35.163
you know, who came in the league
and not have a resume.

00:07:35.163 --> 00:07:36.956
For me, I want, you know, younger kids

00:07:36.956 --> 00:07:40.793
to, you know, when they're growin' up and be able to look at Bones Hyland and his resume,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.753
'I wanna be like Bones
and even better than Bones.'

00:07:42.753 --> 00:07:46.674
So, this is what I'm doin' it for, for
the next generation and things like that.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:49.719
And also, you know, for myself
and wantin' to keep betterin' myself.

00:07:49.886 --> 00:07:53.723
- What do you want people to think about when they say Bones. When they say Bones

00:07:53.723 --> 00:07:57.602
Hyland, what do you want people first think about?
- He's a joyful, impactful kid

00:07:57.643 --> 00:08:01.481
who can put a smile on anybody
face at any given time, any given moment.

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:03.024
That's what I always live by.

00:08:03.024 --> 00:08:05.359
- I really appreciate you
taking the time with us. OK?

00:08:05.359 --> 00:08:07.069
Yes sir, appreciate you.
- Thank you so much. Yes, yes.

